WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.609
A core metacognitive skill is knowing what you know, what you partly know, and what you do not know yet.

2
00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:10.653
This sounds simple, but it is often difficult.

3
00:00:10.653 --> 00:00:19.403
People can feel confident because a topic is familiar, because the explanation sounded clear, or because they have seen the same words before.

4
00:00:19.403 --> 00:00:24.349
That feeling can be useful, but it is not proof of usable understanding.

5
00:00:24.349 --> 00:00:26.251
Familiarity and mastery are different.

6
00:00:26.251 --> 00:00:28.153
Familiarity means something feels recognizable.

7
00:00:28.153 --> 00:00:36.523
Mastery means you can explain, retrieve, apply, compare, or adapt the idea when the source is not directly in front of you.

8
00:00:36.523 --> 00:00:44.132
A fluent explanation can hide weak comprehension if the person has not tested the idea through examples, application, or recall.

9
00:00:44.132 --> 00:00:47.937
Confidence can rise faster than competence when feedback is missing.

10
00:00:47.937 --> 00:00:51.361
Illusions of understanding are common in learning and work.

11
00:00:51.361 --> 00:00:58.589
A person may believe they understand a system because they can follow a diagram while someone else explains it.

12
00:00:58.589 --> 00:01:05.437
Later, when asked to troubleshoot the system, they may discover that the relationships were never secure in memory.

13
00:01:05.437 --> 00:01:07.339
The issue is not intelligence.

14
00:01:07.339 --> 00:01:10.383
The issue is that recognition felt like capability.

15
00:01:10.383 --> 00:01:13.807
To test understanding, ask the idea to do work.

16
00:01:13.807 --> 00:01:16.090
Explain it in your own words.

17
00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:18.372
Apply it to a new example.

18
00:01:18.372 --> 00:01:20.275
Retrieve the steps without looking.

19
00:01:20.275 --> 00:01:23.318
Compare two similar cases and explain the difference.

20
00:01:23.318 --> 00:01:25.601
Teach the idea to someone else.

21
00:01:25.601 --> 00:01:33.210
These tests make gaps visible, and visible gaps are useful because they tell you what to learn or practice next.
